Having a tail pointer in a linked list dives you direct access to the last node in the list. This can be beneficial because you do not need to walk across the entire list to find and reference the end node. Knowing where the end node is makes appending a linked list extremely more efficient because you only need to make one step to complete these tasks, rather than at least n steps to complete the action. Backwards traversal of a doubly linked list is much more efficient with a tail pointer because you have direct access to the previous node and can use the tail as a starting point. 
